welcome to CBS this morning we begin  with this in Iran where the leaders here  face a third day of angry protests after  admitting to a deadly attack on a  commercial airliner security forces  fired tear gas at demonstrators  unconfirmed reports say live bullets  were also fired at the crowd in Tehran  protesters tore down posters of General  Kasim Soleimani killed by the u.s.  earlier this month President Trump  tweeted yesterday to the leaders of Iran  do not kill your protestors Iran says  the missile attack that downed the  Ukraine International Airlines jet  killing 176 people was a mistake  Elizabeth Palmer is in London after  Iran's government forced her and other  reporters to leave that country over the  weekend  Elizabeth why did they kick you out good  morning well when the Iranian government  asks foreign reporters to leave and it  has happened to me and many others  before it's a sign that they are  expecting public unrest and sure enough  that's exactly what they got  just hours after Iran's military  admitted shooting down the passenger  plane the protests began thousands of  mostly young people joined angry crowds  in Tehran and the protest continued last  night to social media videos showed at  least one woman shot in the leg Tehran's  police chief said he'd urged his  officers to use restraint but witnesses  say police were using live rounds just  not shooting to kill it all started with  a candlelight vigil but quickly morphed  into crowds chanting the Revolutionary  Guards are dictators and the enemy is  not America it's within one social media  post shows students refusing to walk on  large US and Israeli flags painted in  the road many of them will have seen and  shared president Trump's tweets in Farsi  calling on the Iranian leadership not to  kill the protesters on Saturday in a  stunning move general Amir Ali Hodges  adair had told the nation  it was Iran's army that had downed the  plane mistaking it for the US cruise  missile  but as pictures of the wreckage  circulated an international  investigators started their work  disbelief turned to fury not only on the  streets but everywhere Iranians were  asking if there really had been a threat  of u.s. strikes why hadn't the military  done the obvious thing and closed its  airspace to civilian planes so far today  our contacts in Tehran say things are  calm but there's no doubt public anger  is boiling government officials and the  state media are trying to deflect some  of the blame for the plane crash on to  the United States for killing Qasem  Soleimani and they say forcing Iranian  air defenses onto full alert Tony Luz  Palmer forests in London Liz thank you  very much  you
 

